---
title: Retry and backoff
intent: Recover from deterministic transient responses and verify attempt counts.
group: resilience
features:
- retry
command: curlew run testapi/collections/30-retry.yaml --var mud="$MUDFLAT_URL" --var ws="$MUDFLAT_WS_URL" --var run="$RUN_ID"
order: 60
runnable: true
---

Mudflat keeps attempt state under the run namespace. The collection asserts how many attempts occurred instead of trusting an illustrative timing trace. Use a fresh RUN_ID on each execution.

## Prerequisites

Use the complete repository checkout and start Mudflat as described in the [cookbook setup](https://github.com/weiqigod/curlew/blob/main/site/README.md). Run these commands from the repository root with `curlew` on PATH.

```bash
export MUDFLAT_URL="${MUDFLAT_URL:-http://127.0.0.1:18080}"
export MUDFLAT_WS_URL="${MUDFLAT_WS_URL:-ws://127.0.0.1:18080}"
export RUN_ID="cookbook-$(date +%s)-$$"
```

## Run it

```bash
curlew run testapi/collections/30-retry.yaml --var mud="$MUDFLAT_URL" --var ws="$MUDFLAT_WS_URL" --var run="$RUN_ID"
```

A successful execution exits 0. Read assertion outcomes as well as the exit code.
No example output or timing numbers are fabricated here.

## Complete collection

<!-- cookbook-source: testapi/collections/30-retry.yaml -->
```yaml
# Retry, against failures that are deterministic rather than hoped-for.
#
# Attempt counters live on the server and key on (session, endpoint, query), so
# "fails twice then succeeds" means exactly that, every run.
name: Retry against deterministic failures
description: |
  Exercises retry triggers, backoff strategies, network-error classification and
  Retry-After handling. Delays are kept small so the suite stays fast; the
  behaviour under test is which attempt succeeds, not how long it waited.

requests:
  - name: recovers after two 503s
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-retry/flaky/fail-then-succeed?times=2&status=503"
    retry:
      enabled: true
      max_attempts: 4
      initial_delay_ms: 10
      backoff_strategy: constant
      retry_on:
        status_codes: [503]
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        # Attempt 3 is the first success. A retry that fired the wrong number of
        # times lands on a different number here, which is a far sharper signal
        # than a pass or fail alone.
        $.attempt: { equals: 3 }
        $.failing: { equals: false }

  - name: status range shorthand triggers a retry
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-retry/flaky/fail-then-succeed?times=1&status=502"
    retry:
      enabled: true
      max_attempts: 3
      initial_delay_ms: 10
      backoff_strategy: constant
      retry_on:
        status_ranges: ["5xx"]
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.attempt: { equals: 2 }

  - name: exponential backoff reaches the success attempt
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-retry/flaky/fail-then-succeed?times=3&status=503&tag=exp"
    retry:
      enabled: true
      max_attempts: 5
      initial_delay_ms: 5
      max_delay_ms: 50
      backoff_strategy: exponential
      jitter: true
      jitter_factor: 0.2
      retry_on:
        status_codes: [503]
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.attempt: { equals: 4 }

  - name: recovery followed by another failure
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-retry/flaky/fail-on?attempts=1,2&status=503"
    retry:
      enabled: true
      max_attempts: 4
      initial_delay_ms: 10
      backoff_strategy: constant
      retry_on:
        status_codes: [503]
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.attempt: { equals: 3 }

  - name: do_not_retry_on wins over retry_on
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-retry/flaky/fail-then-succeed?times=5&status=503&tag=excluded"
    retry:
      enabled: true
      max_attempts: 4
      initial_delay_ms: 10
      backoff_strategy: constant
      retry_on:
        status_ranges: ["5xx"]
      do_not_retry_on:
        status_codes: [503]
    assertions:
      # 503 appears in both lists. The specification says exclusion always wins,
      # so this must fail on the first attempt without retrying.
      status: 503
      body:
        $.attempt: { equals: 1 }

  - name: a network reset is retried
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-retry/flaky/reset-then-succeed?times=1"
    retry:
      enabled: true
      max_attempts: 3
      initial_delay_ms: 10
      backoff_strategy: constant
      retry_on:
        network_errors: true
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.attempt: { equals: 2 }

  - name: retry-after in delta-seconds
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/retry-after/seconds?n=1"
    assertions:
      status: 429
      headers:
        Retry-After: { equals: "1" }
      body:
        $.retry_after: { equals: "1" }

  - name: retry-after as an HTTP-date
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/retry-after/date?ms=1000"
    assertions:
      status: 429
      headers:
        Retry-After: { matches: "GMT$" }

  - name: an oversized retry-after is still just a header
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/retry-after/huge"
    assertions:
      # Retry is off here on purpose. With retry enabled and no clamp this waits
      # an hour, which is what the Phase 2 gap harness will assert.
      status: 429
      headers:
        Retry-After: { equals: "3600" }

  - name: a malformed retry-after is passed through
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/retry-after/malformed"
    assertions:
      status: 429
      headers:
        Retry-After: { equals: "soon" }
```
